From faf66e3c28739d62e13686afafe244ef684c0471 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Orsavio P. Delmare" <orsavio@delmare.lu>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 23:41:47 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 48/50] docs: correct the ECW336 board, and note machid is a
 DTB selector not a product ID

My earlier note here said machid=8010006 meant "ap-hk07". Wrong, and the
correction is more useful than the original claim.

u-boot prints the decode itself: "CDT version:2, Platform ID:8, Major ID:1,
Minor ID:0, Subtype:6". machid is those four CDT fields packed into four
bytes, and the HK01 revision DTBs map C<N> to subtype N, so subtype 6 selects
hk01.c6 (AP-HK01-C6) -- which is what userspace prints as "Platform Name:
ap-hk01-c6". ap-hk07 is a different board, SoC id pair 0x156 0x185, and is
not that hardware.

The stronger evidence for the original point: the ECW336's kernel FIT is a
multi-board blob carrying about 25 DTBs (hk01 through hk14, ac0x, oak0x) and
defaults to config@hk08, which matches neither its board nor the DTB it
actually boots. Nothing in such an image identifies the product.

The catch that matters for this port: machid is not a product ID. The
EWS377-FIT unit recorded in hardware-variants reports the same machid=8010006
as the ECW336, differing only in hw_id (0101012B vs 01010123). Senao
evidently programs one CDT across its IPQ807x line, so a dual-band EWS377-FIT
and a tri-band 6E ECW336 land on the same reference DTB while being obviously
different products. Added a table splitting the questions -- machid for DTB
selection, hw_id and the header's product_id for the product, inspection for
peripherals -- and updated blocker 1 so the ECW230v3 check reads hw_id too
and expects the same machid as the EWS377 units rather than something
hk07/hk08-specific.
